win32u: Give another process's child window its top-level's dpi.

What he saw: FL Studio 2026's TNumBar and TVectorPanel children reported 96 dpi inside its 168 dpi window when
listed from another process; the panels themselves look normal.

Mechanism: get_win_monitor_dpi walks up to the top-level for windows of its own process, but a window of another
process has no WND here (WND_OTHER_PROCESS), so it took that window's OWN rect. FL's mixer and event-editor panels
run past the right edge of its maximized window; asked from another process, those children answered with the dpi of
the screen their clipped-off part lies over (192 on the lab's 200% second screen, SOLID; the 96 first reported, INFERRED, came from a 100% one). In FL's own
process they report 168 (lab probe, same-process vs other-process on the same windows). The same path serves (INFERRED,
untested) a plugin window FL's bridge process parents into FL's windows, and cross-process GetWindowRect mapping.
Upstream Wine 11.10 does the same.

Windows behavior: one dpi for a window tree, the top-level's, whoever asks (INFERRED; Windows probe dpichild.exe is
waiting, needs two monitors at different scales).

Change: for WND_OTHER_PROCESS, take the rect of NtUserGetAncestor( GA_ROOT ) instead. Only the default walk
(NEUTRON_DPI_WALK) has it; NEUTRON_DPI_WALK=0 restores the old recursive code wholesale.

Not covered: mixed-hosting children whose awareness differs from their top-level's.

Lab evidence (desk-g, 175% + 200%): dpichild.exe other-process query of children over the 200% screen: 11.10-181d
168 in 7 of 8 runs (one empty run, see the child-create-dpi patch), NEUTRON_CHILD_DPI_ROOT=0 192 in 2 of 2, stock
11.10-181 192 in 3 of 3. FL Studio 2026 listed from another process: 181 = 65 windows 168 + 3 at 192 (TNumBar,
two TVectorPanel); 181d = all 68 at 168, identical rects; FL's look unchanged.

Kill switch: NEUTRON_CHILD_DPI_ROOT=0.


--- a/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
@@ -3238,6 +3238,30 @@
     return ret;
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON child-dpi-root: a window of another process has no WND here, so get_win_monitor_dpi took ITS OWN rect.
+ * For a child that is wrong: FL Studio's mixer panels run past the right edge of its maximized 175% window, and
+ * asked from another process (or by a plugin window FL's bridge parents into them) they reported the dpi of the
+ * 200% screen beside it, 192 instead of 168; in-process they report 168. Windows keeps one dpi for the window tree,
+ * the top-level's, whoever asks. Only the walk (the default, NEUTRON_DPI_WALK) has it. Kill switch
+ * NEUTRON_CHILD_DPI_ROOT=0. */
+static BOOL neutron_child_dpi_root(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    if (on < 0)
+    {
+        const char *e = getenv( "NEUTRON_CHILD_DPI_ROOT" );
+        on = !e || e[0] != '0';
+        if (!on) ERR( "neutron-gateresolved: NEUTRON_CHILD_DPI_ROOT = 0 (environment)\n" );
+    }
+    return on;
+}
+
+static HWND other_process_root( HWND hwnd )
+{
+    HWND root = NtUserGetAncestor( hwnd, GA_ROOT );
+    return root ? root : hwnd;
+}
+
 static UINT get_win_monitor_dpi_recursive( HWND hwnd, UINT *raw_dpi )
 {
     UINT dpi = NTUSER_DPI_CONTEXT_GET_DPI( get_window_dpi_awareness_context( hwnd ) );
@@ -3314,6 +3338,8 @@
         if (win == WND_DESKTOP) return monitor_dpi_from_rect( rect, get_thread_dpi(), raw_dpi );
         if (win == WND_OTHER_PROCESS)
         {
+            /* NEUTRON child-dpi-root: the walk to the top-level stops at another process's window */
+            if (neutron_child_dpi_root()) hwnd = other_process_root( hwnd );
             dpi = NTUSER_DPI_CONTEXT_GET_DPI( get_window_dpi_awareness_context( hwnd ) );
             if (!get_window_rect( hwnd, &rect, dpi )) return 0;
             return monitor_dpi_from_rect( rect, dpi, raw_dpi );
